abstract: >-
  I didn't invent a machine. I found one. That is my conviction. Take a blank
  cell and write a 0 in it, and keep it a 0: something is now the case that
  wasn't, and that moment, nothing becoming something, is never free. It
  gives off heat by happening, in any real device, and keeping it is paid on
  top, in a merge, which carries Landauer's floor (a named premise) when the
  merged states were really possible, or in room. Certification, a record
  that a claim was checked and closed, is the sharpest case. The Thiele
  Machine is an abstract model of computation, a machine the way Turing's is,
  and this work is an argument made with it about which distinctions an
  account of computation should preserve. It is not a CPU, an instruction set,
  or category theory. The machine here is a witness, not the subject: a
  minimal two-counter machine whose commitments are earned, where every
  certified run from a clean start contains a passing check of the committed
  claim, a commitment to that same claim, and a certificate, and costs at
  least three. Thiele-complete asks for four things that say nothing about
  what the record is about: a universal base whose moves cost no marks and
  cannot touch the record, a record that stays up, blank starts, and a run
  whose record ends up from one start and down from another. Every clock
  fails it, every universal base reaches it by adding marks, and every
  Thiele-complete machine hides its record and its ledger from its own
  window. Certified Thiele-complete is exactly Thiele-complete with clean
  starts, the earned check, commitment and certificate of one claim, and a
  claim that can fail added; the small machine meets it. A universal machine U, one fixed program of
  the small machine's own instructions, runs every program of the small
  machine from every start, halts exactly when its guest halts, raises its
  flag by its own check, commit and certificate exactly when the guest would,
  and keeps the guest's ledger mark for mark where the runs line up. A
  computation gives you an answer. What did it establish on the way there?
  Which distinctions did it keep? What did it cost to establish them?
  Certification is the case I could pin down. Its proofs show exactly what
  specified observations lose. A full encoding can preserve those
  distinctions; the question is which ones a model should keep. The formal
  development includes accounting laws for systems satisfying A2, pricing
  adequacy for designated events, observation adequacy, and a finite family
  of unit event floors that share one charge. Retaining history makes a
  deterministic step injective without changing its observed transition, by
  paying room at every step. On a finite state space the room runs out: a
  certificate no step revokes
  can only be switched on by a step that merges states, so pricing merges,
  as Landauer's principle does in the worst case over what the machine might
  hold, yields A2; the heat floor comes from uncertainty about the state, so under
  Landauer's principle a known state owes no floor, though the step still
  gives off heat when it happens and the distinction is held by whoever
  knows the start. Priced per halving, switching
  k states on beside m certified ones costs at least log2((m+k)/m), and on
  finite hardware that prices merges, whose instructions have decidable
  equality, the events that must be priced are exactly the merges, with every
  permanent-record write among them. The same bound holds in bits of Shannon
  entropy from the uniform distribution on those m + k states, and an
  eight-state machine meets both premises as theorems. An exact price for certification cannot be
  computed from a window in which a certifying step and a non-certifying one
  look alike. Three results carry the weight of the structural question:
  every deterministic machine can carry the record axis, as itself plus a
  latch on an event it reaches; every Thiele-complete machine hides its
  record from its own window; and on finite hardware a step that writes a
  permanent record merges states, so pricing merges prices the write. A
  record that never switches off and is driven by the base computation is a
  latch on one event; both premises are essential. Which event is latched is
  the pointer question; no theorem selects one uniquely. Consensus, exact
  observation, and coordinator-free evolution do not force permanence; the
  pointer criterion is a thesis. Finite growing records decompose into
  threshold latches, although a single-latch decomposition is false. Scoped
  RFC 9162, PCC, addressed-RAM, reversible-update, and TPM-interface results
  delimit the real-system claims. The lambda calculus L has its
  recursion theorem and Rice's theorem, proved from its reduction rules; the local
  cost-semantics correspondences are not full cost-framework embeddings. The
  two-state calorimeter protocol computes exact heat while proving that its
  population dynamics do not determine the energy scale. Physical
  calibration requires thermal and device premises. The observer-map
  counterexample is model-dependent. No surveyed external consequence
  satisfied all four novelty and applicability criteria. Those are
  mathematical answers with explicit premises. The repository contains the
  Coq development, the small machine, the monograph, and the mathematical
  specification; an earlier virtual machine and hardware development is
  preserved at tag v3.3.0. The assumption receipt covers 6,142
  statements across 351 files: 5,322 are closed under the global context, 820 depend on
  standard-library axioms, and none depend on project-local or third-party
  axioms. The receipt generator writes these counts on Linux. Paid
  certification alone establishes accounting; semantic truth requires a sound
  checker, and on the small machine every certificate rests on one. The
  physical and foundational interpretations are not claims of the formal
  corpus. The software is licensed Apache-2.0. The monograph and
  distillation are licensed CC-BY-SA-4.0.
